ught to gratify thy vindictive temper, in punishing my people. (Calmet) — The sins of both called down vengeance. (Worthington)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Lady. Pride goes before ruin, Proverbs xvi. 18."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Two. The empire and the people shall be removed at once. — Enchanters; princes or magicians, who gave them evil counsel, ver. 12."],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Know. All this shews the vanity of magic, which cannot announce future events to do any good. (Calmet)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Months, to tell which would prove lucky, Esther iii. 7."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Thereat, to warm themselves, (Haydock) or to adore. (Calmet) — In Cappadocia are to be seen “Pyratheia,…in which the magi keep a perpetual fire, and sing hymns about the space of an hour.” (Strabo xv.) — These were a sort of open temples. (Calmet)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Merchants. The city was well situated for trade, chap. xiii. 20. (Diodorus ii.)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}